course: english
lesson: 11
title: Homophones and near-homophones
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Homophones are words that sound the same but are spelled differently and mean different things: see and sea, bear and bare, piece and peace. When you read, the spelling tells you which one; when you write, you have to choose, so homophones are spelling traps, not reading traps. The meaning of the sentence decides: «I see the sea» needs both.
explain: The most famous trio is there, their and they're. «There» is a place (it has «here» inside it), «their» means belonging to them, and «they're» is short for «they are». The test is simple: can you say «they are» instead? Then it is «they're». «There going to the park» fails the test and should be «They're going to the park».
explain: Apostrophes cause the worst muddles. «It's» means «it is» or «it has»; «its» means belonging to it, with no apostrophe, just like his and hers. So «The dog wagged its tail». The same goes for «you're» (you are) and «your» (belonging to you), and «who's» (who is) and «whose» (belonging to whom). Say the long form; if it fits, use the apostrophe.
explain: Some pairs only sound nearly the same: near-homophones. «Accept» means to take or agree («accept the gift»); «except» means not including. «Affect» is usually the doing word (the rain affected the match), «effect» usually the thing (the rain had an effect). Grown-ups mix these up too; shop signs saying «your welcome» are everywhere.
explain: Slang can give an old word a new meaning. In London youth slang «bare» means very or lots of: «That party was bare good». It is not wrong, just a different place: playground or homework. And for «to, too, two»: «to» goes towards or before a verb, «too» means also or very (it has too many o's), «two» is the number 2.
fact: Homophones sound the same but have different spellings and meanings: see/sea, bear/bare, piece/peace.
fact: «There» is a place, «their» means belonging to them, «they're» means «they are».
fact: «To» shows direction or comes before a verb; «too» means also or very; «two» is the number 2.
fact: «Your» means belonging to you; «you're» means «you are».
fact: «Its» means belonging to it; «it's» means «it is» or «it has».
fact: «Whose» asks who something belongs to; «who's» means «who is» or «who has».
fact: A contraction is a short form with an apostrophe where letters are missing: they're, you're, it's, who's.
fact: «Bear» is the animal or means to carry; «bare» means uncovered.
fact: «Piece» is a part of something; «peace» is calm, with no fighting.
fact: A trick for «piece»: «a piece of pie», both start with p-i-e.
fact: Homophones stick best when you learn them inside sentences, like «I see the sea».
fact: «Accept» means to take or agree; «except» means not including.
fact: «Affect» is usually a verb (to change something); «effect» is usually a noun (the result).
fact: The sentence around a homophone, its context, tells you which spelling is needed.
fact: Slang in this lesson: in London youth slang «bare» means very or lots of; «eejit» is Irish for idiot; «wee» is Scottish and Northern Irish for small.
exercise: Mia writes «There going to the park». What should it be? | They're | Their | Thier | Thei're
exercise: Which test tells you to use «they're»? | You can say «they are» instead | It is about a place | It is about what they own | It starts a sentence
exercise: Jack writes «I want to come to». What should the last word be? | too | to | two | toe
exercise: Uncle in Galway sees a sign saying «your welcome». What should it say? | you're welcome | yore welcome | your welcom | you'are welcome
exercise: Ellie writes «The dog wagged it's tail». What is right? | its | it's | its' | it is
exercise: Raj writes about a forest «bare». Which word means the animal? | bear | bare | baer | bair
exercise: Abbie says the party was «bare good». What does «bare» mean in London slang? | Very | Without clothes | Empty | Not very
exercise: «I can ___ the sea.» Which word fits? | see | sea | si | seae
exercise: Sam writes «affect» for the result of the rain. Which word means the result? | effect | affect | afect | effict
exercise: Mum says «accept the gift». What does «accept» mean? | To take or agree | Leaving something out | To throw it in the bin | To swap it for another
exercise: Ciara in Belfast asks «piece» or «peace» of cake. Which one? | piece | peace | peice | pees
exercise: Leon writes «Whose going to the match?». What is right? | Who's | Whos | Who'se | Whose'
exercise: Which word is a place and has «here» inside it? | there | their | they're | three
exercise: What is a homophone? | A word that sounds like another but has a different spelling and meaning | A word that means the same as another but sounds completely different | A word spelled backwards | A very long word that is hard to say
exercise: What is «two»? | The number 2 | Also, or very | Towards a place | A kind of shoe
exercise: Who usually mixes up homophones on signs? | Grown-ups as well as children | Only small children | Only people who can't read | Nobody, signs get checked
test: «ur welcome, mate, it's grand». Uncle says the sign's wrong. Why? | It needs «you're», because it means «you are» | It needs «your», because it's about you | It needs «yore», the old spelling from long ago | Nothing is wrong with «your welcome»
test: «the apostrophe means belonging, so it's tail must be right innit». Put her right. | «Its» for belonging; «it's» only means «it is» or «it has» | She's right, the apostrophe always shows belonging | Both «its» and «it's» are fine for belonging, as long as the reader understands | «Its'» with the apostrophe at the end is right
test: What would happen if we spelled «there», «their» and «they're» all the same? | Readers would have to guess the meaning from the sentence | Nothing, nobody ever needs the spelling | All three words would mean «a place» | People would stop using them and say «they are» every time
test: «ugh, I learned the homophone list and still got «I can sea the see» wrong». What would help? | Learn homophones in sentences, like «I see the sea» | Learn the list again, louder | Only ever use one of each pair | Skip any sentence with homophones
test: «a wee question, is it a peace of cake?» What is the trick? | «A piece of pie»: both have «pie» | «Peace» has «pea», like food | Both spellings mean the same | «Peace» is for cake, «piece» for pie
test: «that party was bare good» on a homework story. What is the wise advice? | Fine with friends; in homework write «very good» | It is a spelling mistake for «bear» | It means the party had no people | «Bare» can never mean «very»
test: A cartoon bear holds a sign: «Bare crossing». What is wrong? | It means uncovered; the animal is «bear» | Nothing, bare is the animal | It should say «bair» | «Bear» is only a verb, to carry
test: «I can't except your apology». What should it be? | accept, meaning to take or agree | except, meaning leaving something out | expect, meaning to wait for something to happen | excerpt, meaning a short part of a text
test: «whose going to the shop? nan's car's outside». What's the slip? | It should be «who's», meaning «who is» | It should be «whos», no apostrophe | Nothing, «whose» means «who is» | It should be «hoose», the Scots way
test: ★★★ How many mistakes in: «Their going too the park with there dog»? | 3 | 2 | 1 | 4
test: ★★★ Which sentence is spelled correctly? | It's raining, so the cat licked its paws | Its raining, so the cat licked it's paws | It's raining, so the cat licked it's paws | Its raining, so the cat licked its paws
test: ★★★ «The noise had a big ___ on the dogs; it ___ them all night.» Which pair fits? | effect, affected | affect, effected | effect, effected | affect, affected
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a homophone? | A word identical in pronunciation to another but distinct in spelling and meaning | A word identical in meaning to another but distinct in pronunciation | A word of which the spelling is identical when reversed, as in «level» or «noon» | A word employed exclusively in poetry
exam: Ascertain the correct form to be employed in the sentence «___ going to the park», wherein the meaning is «they are». | They're | Their | There | They'are
exam: Identify the principal distinction between «its» and «it's». | «Its» denotes possession, whereas «it's» is a contraction of «it is» or «it has» | «It's» denotes possession, whereas «its» is a contraction of «it is» | The two forms are interchangeable in all written English, whether formal or informal, and in all countries | «Its» is the British form and «it's» the American form of the same word
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the words «accept» and «except». | «Accept» signifies to receive or agree, whereas «except» signifies «not including» | Both words signify «not including» and may be used interchangeably | «Except» signifies to receive, whereas «accept» signifies to exclude | «Accept» is employed only in formal correspondence
